WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.518 --> 00:00:03.351
(crowd clamoring)

00:00:09.766 --> 00:00:13.099
(acoustic guitar music)

00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:22.540
- The garment industry is broken.

00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:24.719
People would be shocked

00:00:24.719 --> 00:00:28.289
if they saw what goes
on in their factories.

00:00:28.289 --> 00:00:30.740
It really chances the way
you think about clothing,

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:34.720
when you consider the
people in the process.

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:38.580
But because there's no
visibility when you buy garments,

00:00:38.580 --> 00:00:41.412
you are just oblivious to it.

00:00:41.412 --> 00:00:42.572
- [Newscaster] The Rana Plaza complex,

00:00:42.572 --> 00:00:44.793
which housed a textile
factory, collapsed last week,

00:00:44.793 --> 00:00:47.025
killing 400.
- Rescue workers

00:00:47.025 --> 00:00:48.932
in the Bangladeshi capital Dhaka

00:00:48.932 --> 00:00:50.865
are continuing to hunt for survivors.

00:00:50.865 --> 00:00:52.257
- [Newscaster] Today's disaster

00:00:52.257 --> 00:00:54.718
is unlikely to slow a booming industry

00:00:54.718 --> 00:00:57.831
which thrives on the
world's lowest labor costs.

00:00:57.831 --> 00:01:01.231
- When factories collapse,
we hear about it.

00:01:01.231 --> 00:01:04.162
When factory fires kill
workers, we hear about it.

00:01:04.162 --> 00:01:07.871
- The grinding long days,
the difficulties they face,

00:01:07.871 --> 00:01:09.682
we don't hear about that.

00:01:09.682 --> 00:01:12.930
It touches something we don't
want to talk about a lot,

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:17.220
which is, poorly paid
people living in places

00:01:17.220 --> 00:01:20.068
where they don't have
a lot of opportunity.

00:01:20.068 --> 00:01:23.068
That's a hard subject to talk about.

00:01:25.359 --> 00:01:28.692
(acoustic guitar music)

00:01:30.849 --> 00:01:34.948
- The root of the problem
is corporate greed.

00:01:34.948 --> 00:01:37.537
It's companies trying to maximize profit

00:01:37.537 --> 00:01:40.449
by selling as much, for
as little as possible,

00:01:40.449 --> 00:01:43.999
to consumers who are willing
to buy those products.

00:01:43.999 --> 00:01:45.009
- More and more Americans are awakening

00:01:45.009 --> 00:01:47.908
to the reality that there are responsible,

00:01:47.908 --> 00:01:52.548
sustainable alternatives
to sweatshop products.

00:01:52.548 --> 00:01:55.318
- Your purchasing decisions
can affect the lives

00:01:55.318 --> 00:01:56.827
of workers around the world.

00:01:56.827 --> 00:01:58.807
We look at fair trade as the first step

00:01:58.807 --> 00:02:00.945
in a very long journey towards getting

00:02:00.945 --> 00:02:03.412
wage equity in the supply chain.

00:02:03.412 --> 00:02:07.579
(acoustic guitar music ends,
last note reverberates)

00:02:12.201 --> 00:02:16.284
(speaking in a foreign language)

00:02:21.785 --> 00:02:25.952
(men's choir singing
in a foreign language)

00:02:36.265 --> 00:02:40.348
(speaking in a foreign language)

00:03:23.620 --> 00:03:27.370
(bus rumbling over the road)

00:03:49.159 --> 00:03:52.076
(machines humming)

00:03:59.289 --> 00:04:01.740
- My name is Chamara De Silva.

00:04:01.740 --> 00:04:03.713
I'm the general manager
of Hirdaramani Mihila.

00:04:03.713 --> 00:04:07.880
We are making mainly tops,
jackets, t-shirts, pants.

00:04:09.561 --> 00:04:12.201
We are unloading the fabric.

00:04:12.201 --> 00:04:13.589
It's from the fabric supplier

00:04:13.589 --> 00:04:15.172
that's from Taiwan.

00:04:16.060 --> 00:04:18.393
This will spread the fabric.

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:21.801
They will drive the cutter for the design.

00:04:21.801 --> 00:04:24.884
So after cutting, it will be numbered

00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:30.570
and then it will be inspected.

00:04:30.570 --> 00:04:35.040
There are 1,400 people
working in my factory.

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:39.123
It's about 78% women and about 22% men.

00:04:40.882 --> 00:04:42.611
Piece by piece, one person will be doing

00:04:42.611 --> 00:04:44.194
only one operation.

00:04:46.022 --> 00:04:49.222
She checks the garment,
whether it is good or bad.

00:04:49.222 --> 00:04:50.805
98.8% is good.

00:04:53.439 --> 00:04:56.531
At the end of the line, packed in box,

00:04:56.531 --> 00:04:58.781
the completed goods go out.

00:04:59.860 --> 00:05:02.291
- So there are a lot
of factories out there

00:05:02.291 --> 00:05:03.530
that do an amazing job.

00:05:03.530 --> 00:05:04.792
Right now the problem
is that those factories

00:05:04.792 --> 00:05:07.672
that are performing at
a more sustainable level

00:05:07.672 --> 00:05:10.331
simply don't have an effective way

00:05:10.331 --> 00:05:12.171
to compete with the bottom-feeders,

00:05:12.171 --> 00:05:13.992
with the bad factories.

00:05:13.992 --> 00:05:17.854
Bad labor practices look
pretty much the same.

00:05:17.854 --> 00:05:21.363
A common theme is bad
treatment of workers,

00:05:21.363 --> 00:05:24.323
low wages, unsafe factories, and pollution

00:05:24.323 --> 00:05:27.190
of the environment around those factories.

00:05:27.190 --> 00:05:29.649
We live in the age of globalization.

00:05:29.649 --> 00:05:33.960
Farmers and factory
workers around the world

00:05:33.960 --> 00:05:36.097
are going to be plugged
into the global market

00:05:36.097 --> 00:05:37.418
in one way or another

00:05:37.418 --> 00:05:38.779
and so for me the question is,

00:05:38.779 --> 00:05:42.518
are they victims of the global market?

00:05:42.518 --> 00:05:44.807
Or are they being included in the benefits

00:05:44.807 --> 00:05:46.224
of globalization?

00:05:50.539 --> 00:05:51.488
- Fair trade in the garment industry

00:05:51.488 --> 00:05:53.120
is a fairly new concept.

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:55.262
Typically it's associated
with other products,

00:05:55.262 --> 00:05:57.929
like chocolate, tea, and coffee.

00:05:58.990 --> 00:06:01.371
For a factory to become
Fair Trade Certified,

00:06:01.371 --> 00:06:03.521
the factory works hard towards meeting

00:06:03.521 --> 00:06:04.881
Fair Trade standards.

00:06:04.881 --> 00:06:06.121
When brands join the program, they agree

00:06:06.121 --> 00:06:07.229
to pay a premium on top

00:06:07.229 --> 00:06:09.089
of the cost of the item.

00:06:09.089 --> 00:06:10.918
- The good news is those workers receive

00:06:10.918 --> 00:06:11.758
that money directly.

00:06:11.758 --> 00:06:13.009
It doesn't even go through management.

00:06:13.009 --> 00:06:14.209
The workers set up their own bank account,

00:06:14.209 --> 00:06:16.038
they manage those funds directly,

00:06:16.038 --> 00:06:19.438
they get to vote on whether
or not to distribute

00:06:19.438 --> 00:06:23.605
that money as a bonus or
start a scholarship program

00:06:24.478 --> 00:06:26.645
or build a daycare center.

00:06:32.555 --> 00:06:35.336
- We are working with about eight brands

00:06:35.336 --> 00:06:36.777
in this Mihila factory.

00:06:36.777 --> 00:06:39.097
Patagonia were the one and only brand

00:06:39.097 --> 00:06:40.678
approved for fair trade.

00:06:40.678 --> 00:06:42.277
So we learned about the fair trade

00:06:42.277 --> 00:06:44.467
and then we discussed what this is,

00:06:44.467 --> 00:06:47.199
what are the benefits,
what is the purpose.

00:06:47.199 --> 00:06:48.315
Impression was really superb.

00:06:48.315 --> 00:06:52.755
(speaking a foreign language)

00:06:52.755 --> 00:06:55.126
We met here with the employee council,

00:06:55.126 --> 00:06:57.934
and one of the most, most important proposals

00:06:57.934 --> 00:06:59.267
was the daycare.

00:07:02.233 --> 00:07:05.353
I can remember the day I
went to see the daycare

00:07:05.353 --> 00:07:09.270
because it was one of the
happiest moments in my life.

00:07:10.982 --> 00:07:12.454
I mean, it was amazing, you know.

00:07:12.454 --> 00:07:15.465
I saw the laughter, I saw the smiles.

00:07:15.465 --> 00:07:16.298
(children singing)

00:07:16.298 --> 00:07:19.523
More than anyone, mothers
were really happy.

00:07:19.523 --> 00:07:21.563
They can concentrate on work because

00:07:21.563 --> 00:07:26.262
they know that their
children are in safe hands.

00:07:26.262 --> 00:07:30.262
- (speaking a foreign language)

00:07:40.282 --> 00:07:44.680
- The industry has an
opportunity to take a stance

00:07:44.680 --> 00:07:47.662
and try to provide a better,
more positive way of living

00:07:47.662 --> 00:07:49.682
for a lot of people around the world.

00:07:49.682 --> 00:07:51.323
- We have to prove the business case

00:07:51.323 --> 00:07:52.481
for responsible business.

00:07:52.481 --> 00:07:54.814
If it's just a philanthropic

00:07:56.581 --> 00:07:58.914
or a philosophical endeavor,

00:07:59.962 --> 00:08:00.933
it won't scale.

00:08:00.933 --> 00:08:02.682
We have to prove that fair trade

00:08:02.682 --> 00:08:03.981
is good for business.

00:08:03.981 --> 00:08:06.901
- Patagonia is really ramping
up our program quickly.

00:08:06.901 --> 00:08:10.111
In fall '14, we had 11 fair
trade certified styles.

00:08:10.111 --> 00:08:12.431
In fall '16, we're gonna be at 200,

00:08:12.431 --> 00:08:16.440
and in fall '17, we're
gonna be at 300 styles,

00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:19.773
and that's 30% of our product line.

00:08:22.864 --> 00:08:26.197
(studious violin music)

00:08:58.304 --> 00:09:01.733
- We envision farmers and workers becoming

00:09:01.733 --> 00:09:03.935
architects of their own future.

00:09:03.935 --> 00:09:05.033
We're helping workers not just improve

00:09:05.033 --> 00:09:08.474
their lives but also find their voice.

00:09:08.474 --> 00:09:12.833
The results go way beyond
just $30,000 a year

00:09:12.833 --> 00:09:15.044
to this factory or $50,000 to that factory

00:09:15.044 --> 00:09:16.794
and the projects that they do with that

00:09:16.794 --> 00:09:18.674
but underneath that and above that is

00:09:18.674 --> 00:09:22.007
this invisible dividend from Fair Trade.

00:09:23.781 --> 00:09:26.198
It's all about hope and pride

00:09:27.758 --> 00:09:28.675
and dignity

00:09:29.585 --> 00:09:31.252
and self-confidence.

00:09:32.895 --> 00:09:36.645
(pensive instrumental music)

00:09:43.057 --> 00:09:47.224
(reading aloud in a foreign language)

00:09:53.798 --> 00:09:57.881
(speaking in a foreign language)

00:10:08.412 --> 00:10:11.495
(vehicle honks horn)

00:10:37.994 --> 00:10:42.077
(cheerful acoustic guitar music)

00:10:50.458 --> 00:10:54.291
(speaking a foreign language)

00:11:06.088 --> 00:11:07.698
- In a world where two billion people live

00:11:07.698 --> 00:11:10.727
on two dollars a day,
we clearly have our work

00:11:10.727 --> 00:11:11.977
cut out for us.

00:11:13.476 --> 00:11:15.338
- [Cara Chacon] I think
it's really easy for people

00:11:15.338 --> 00:11:17.915
to forget that there are
people behind everything

00:11:17.915 --> 00:11:19.826
that we buy in the supply chain

00:11:19.826 --> 00:11:21.614
and that hands actually touch it.

00:11:21.614 --> 00:11:24.095
We're not just here to make a product.

00:11:24.095 --> 00:11:26.415
It's about sending a message to the world

00:11:26.415 --> 00:11:29.424
that eventually no factory has workers

00:11:29.424 --> 00:11:30.992
that are exploited.

00:11:30.992 --> 00:11:32.992
I mean, that just should not be tolerated

00:11:32.992 --> 00:11:34.021
in this day and age.

00:11:34.021 --> 00:11:35.973
We need to keep the pressure on so that

00:11:35.973 --> 00:11:37.994
every brand is doing this.

00:11:37.994 --> 00:11:39.913
- [Woman In Blue Shirt] We
just want to sweep the effects

00:11:39.913 --> 00:11:41.863
of thoughtless consumption
under the carpet,

00:11:41.863 --> 00:11:44.471
and we can because we don't
have to think about it.

00:11:44.471 --> 00:11:46.834
That's the invisible story behind

00:11:46.834 --> 00:11:49.369
the products that we buy.

00:11:49.369 --> 00:11:52.540
We have to keep asking the hard questions

00:11:52.540 --> 00:11:54.608
and challenge ourselves.

00:11:54.608 --> 00:11:57.879
There's an easy route
and there's a hard route

00:11:57.879 --> 00:12:00.879
that involves doing the right thing.

00:12:03.834 --> 00:12:07.167
(bright, hopeful music)

